WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.570
Continental extension stretches and thins crust, commonly forming normal faults, tilted

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.890
blocks, and grabens. Magma and warm fluids may accompany rifting.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.990
Extension and the pressure–temperature response vary from place to place during

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.250
the evolution of a rift.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.550
As continental rifting progresses to seafloor spreading, new oceanic crust forms

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.190
at a ridge. Seawater can circulate through hot fractured rock and

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:29.990
cause hydrothermal metamorphism. The hot-water pathway is especially important at mid-ocean

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:30.370
ridges.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.350
A strike-slip fault accommodates primarily horizontal relative motion.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.650
Looking across the fault, the opposite side moves right in a

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.650
right-lateral fault and left in a left-lateral fault.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.050
Offset streams and roads preserve the displacement of originally continuous markers.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.590
A bend or step in a strike-slip system can make adjacent

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.470
rocks converge or pull apart.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.210
Local shortening can build folds and ridges, while local extension can

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.430
produce a basin. The main lateral motion alone does not determine

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.650
the sign of every bend.
